 how we flow money to solve environmental  problems and put us toward a sustainable  future  i think we under attended to that  problem in the 1970s 80s  somehow there was an idea that if the  law was framed the money would follow  turned out not to be true um i also  think there is a need to  focus on integration on a more systems  oriented approach we might call it a  more ecological approach  to these problems and i'm going to dig  in on  all of this uh over the course of the  next 20 minutes or so  as i lay out my argument for why we need  a 21st century approach to environmental  protection  and how this better planet book sketches  uh not only in my essay but in all the  essays  some of the elements of what that might  look like  so first of all i want to be clear that  i'm not saying that the 20th century  strategy  of environmental protection was a  failure quite to the contrary  we have better quality air now much  better quality  rivers for those who pick up the book  you'll see that the introductory chapter  i note that i grew up along the nagaduck  river in northwestern connecticut  and that river used to flow orange and  purple depending on the colors of the  uniroyal factory  that was dumping its waste into the  river the river is much cleaner today  it's swimmable there's fish in it so we  have made  huge progress as a society but i do  think they're important questions about  how we continue to make progress and  address the residual problems  that continue to plague us and that's um  where my argument  focuses on the need for innovation my  belief is we've gotten a good distance  with a certain set of strategies  but to go where we need to go over the  coming years we need new ideas new  approaches  new technologies to be brought into the  sustainability world  and my basic claim  is that the 20th century approach to  environmental protection  was largely around telling people  particularly the business world what  not to do so putting up a red light a  stop sign saying don't do that  don't pollute can't go there and uh that  sort of red light strategy did change  behavior in some regards  but it was not very good at getting  business  to become solutions providers so we  don't have  a transformed energy foundation for our  society compared to what we thought we  might have  in the way of transformation over the  past 50 years  so i want to argue that we should have  just as we have at any intersection  not just red lights that tell people  when to stop but green lights that tell  people when to go  we need the same framework of green  lights as a complement to red lights  in environmental regulation and what i  mean really is that we need incentives  we need to  get people who have the capacity to  solve problems  to step up to those issues to become  solutions providers and i think  my argument is that we need to really  structure incentives  to flow society's resources towards  those solutions providers  and frankly to steer money away from  companies that are a cause of problems  and are not moving us  towards a sustainable future so here's  the  the issues that i see us potentially uh  being able to step  up to um first i think there is a value  in making people pay for the harm they  cause  so the biggest green light is to have  those who are doing things in a clean  way  not polluting not pay and that's a  signal  of strong value in the business world  so again my number one green light is  price  signals and that would tell us if you're  doing things the right way you don't pay  if you're a polluter you're going to pay  for every increment of harm  you're putting out into society and this  is best done if we switch from  the 20th century approach to  environmental protection  it was largely what we in the business  call command  and control regulation uh largely around  government mandates that you can't  exceed a certain level of pollution  but everyone was allowed to pollute up  to that level for free  my argument is nothing for free anymore  either you stop your harm  no pollution in which case you don't  have to pay but if you're continuing to  pollute you're going to pay for every  increment of harm  up to the limit beyond which it's  forbidden and so that's the the core  idea of a price signal  by the way in the better planet book  bill nordhaus our prize-winning nobel  prize-winning  economist colleague lays out the case  for  price signals as the core of the  response to climate change  and i agree now more broadly  my argument is that we should really  seek to and this is the radical part of  the agenda priya promised that i'm  working on some radical ideas  um the big one is that we should really  be committed to  ending what economists would call  externalities  smoke up a smokestack effluent out of  pipeline  any kind of spillover of harm from an  industry or from an individual  onto society more broadly so i think  every company should pay for its  emissions out the smokestack  every one of us should pay for the car  exhaust out the back of our car  uh and i think it was not possible to do  this in the 20th century  because it was much too complicated to  keep track of but our information age  makes that tracking of pollution and the  pricing of harms and the passing out of  pollution bills  are really not a very difficult exercise  at all  and that again if we make people pay for  the harm they're causing  or stop what they're doing that's  causing that problem  that internalizes the externality make  sure that no one is getting  the benefit of putting harms out onto  society  for free by the way this will change  some business models  dramatically because there are some  companies that couldn't survive  except for the fact they are privatizing  the gains of their activities the  products they sell  but externalizing the costs putting at  least in part  those burdens whether in the form of  pollution or something else  onto the community onto the society more  generally  so i am arguing that we need a new  environmental protection agency  not spending its time with engineers  figuring out  what the best available technology is  and that's the core of our current law  and ordering various industries to apply  a certain smokestack scrubber or other  piece of pollution control equipment  onto their factory activity but rather  to have an epa focused on measuring harm  putting a price on it  by calculating how much risk and  epidemiological or  ecological harm is being caused and then  sending out the bills  making people pay for what they're doing  i think that kind of a structure creates  again  a big incentive to control pollution  so every factory would have a big  incentive to reduce its harm  but also to entrepreneurs and innovators  to provide  new approaches to doing all the things  we need to do that will be much lower  emissions  they will be the ones to bring to the  factories ways to cut their harm and  therefore cut their pollution bills  so i think what it does is it  incentivizes  innovation and drives people to become  solutions providers  now again as i said this connects in a  number of ways  to uh some of the other essays that are  in this book  ken gillingham my colleague in the  environment school one of the rising  star young  environmental economists also makes a  big argument around  the importance of innovation so i'm  excited that his essay lines up so  nicely  with my own uh and of course as i  mentioned bill nordhaus  uh the same thing he's making an  argument around  the importance of a policy framework  that puts a price on causing harm  and his central focus as i mentioned is  on a carbon charge  making people pay for the greenhouse gas  emissions that are contributing to  climate change  but there are a lot of other ideas that  i want to just mention  uh and i guess i i want to pause and say  that both the ken gillingham idea  and the bill nordhaus idea are aimed not  only at changing the behavior of the  polluter  but also inspiring others to help  polluters  stop polluting so it's really about not  just incentivizing more  care with the production activities of  society but also to spur  innovation and investment in  energy efficiency in uh clean energy  technology  in renewable power generation uh so  again if that is what can be done  without paying a price there will be a  relative price  signal to steer people away from fossil  fuel burning  towards these new cleaner more renewable  technologies  another source of green lights can be  information  so another part of the 21st century  sustainability strategy that i want to  sketch out  for you is how we use data  and information and the capacity to get  our hands on that information  manage it and make it actionable with  information technology  in ways that were unimaginable a couple  of decades ago  and in our better planet book are  several essays  that talk about how to do this in one  way or another one of the areas that  i've been working on  through the yale initiative on  sustainable finance which i co-direct  with  my school of management colleague todd  court is  centered on better metrics  of corporate sustainability performance  so asking companies to give us more data  on their environmental social and  governance  performance in the marketplace and that  is talked about in the investment world  by its acronym esg reporting  and right now some companies report but  the data is not very good  a lot of it's backward-looking not  forward-looking a lot of it's narrowly  focused on risk and not always focused  on who's a solutions provider therefore  upside opportunities as well as kind of  the downside burdens a company is  causing  a lot of it is very focused on  reputation rather than  operational success so there's a lot of  problems with an existing framework so  todd and i have been arguing and his  essay in the better planet book makes  clear  that we would benefit as a society from  better metrics better data on corporate  performance  really trying to understand who the  corporate sustainability leaders are  and hold them up as a place that  investors might want to go and what we  have found  is that an ever larger number of  investors  are sustainability minded they want  better alignment between their values  and where their portfolios are putting  their money so this idea of better esg  metrics  is very much what they need to make this  desire  for an alignment between their values  and their portfolio  come together so uh i think that's a  great example of where  better data investment and information  and in this case specifically  in a next generation of esg  metrics is going to be essential  now of course some of it still will be  risk oriented  and from a climate change point of view  knowing which companies  are as we now say carbon exposed  likely to be suffering more consequences  than their competitors or those  companies that are in other industries  will be of real value and in particular  companies holding fossil fuel assets may  find themselves  with what are known as stranded assets  oil in the ground  natural gas underground that can't ever  be taken advantage of  because it has become disfavored as we  move to a clean energy future  but the next generation of metrics that  i want to see  goes even beyond those kind of core  elements the things like  which companies and which industries and  which management teams of those  companies  are positioned to transition to a  decarbonized future  because that's what people would really  like to know in the marketplace  looking out ahead 10 or 15 or 20 years  who's positioned  to be a success in business and whose  business model may  fail because it's trapped in the  fossil fuel past now i think that whole  idea  of a different approach for business of  really trying to  understand who the leaders are from a  sustainability perspective  dovetails with another of the essays  that i very much like  uh the one by carter roberts who's the  head of the world wildlife fund  and he has written about kind of the  need for a new business  business ethos and that actually is  capturing a spirit of the moment  suddenly there's a lot of talk that the  old business  mission of thinking that the goal is  always to maximize profits  or to deliver what the famous university  of chicago economist once called  shareholder primacy is giving way  to a new business spirit that focuses on  stakeholder responsibility  and that means that a business is not  just delivering goods  uh and profits for its shareholders its  owners  but has some responsibility to its  employees to the communities it operates  in to its customers to its suppliers  and i think uh that argument very much  is what carter roberts  is uh saying in his essay in this better  planet book  i believe that there is a societal value  change  underway that is basically saying to the  business community  you need to be more broad-minded it  can't simply be  all about profits and certainly not  about profits  where you are privatizing gains and  socializing putting on all of us the  costs of things like your pollution  so better information makes that  possible we've got a new capacity with  data  and information technology to track  who's putting those emissions out  and again in my reimagined world making  those people pay for the harms they're  causing  now it is also the case that with better  data  and tracking we can manage a whole set  of other  sustainability or environmental problems  better and another of the essays that in  the better planet book i find really  inspiring in its uh  nod to where the future might go is the  one by my environment school colleague  marion certo  on waste management and marian some of  you may know has been in that arena  for something like 30 years uh and has  really been  on the front lines of thinking about how  we do recapture and recycling of  materials  and how we try to minimize waste uh and  that's a  booming issue of societal concern uh  obviously around things like plastic but  even more broadly  and so marion lays out with one of her  uh students  a beautiful argument for a price-based  strategy of waste management where you  make people pay for what they throw away  but allow them to recycle for free and  uh  she's got a a well-structured essay  on how it is we might reimagine waste  management to really ensure that we get  to a more  closed-loop use of materials where that  which can be taken back  recaptured and reused will be and where  we're minimizing that which goes out  uh forever as waste likewise there's a  great essay in the book from tracy  meehan  uh and by the way one of the notable  things about this book is  these are not essays from  environmentalists they're from  environmental analysts  so people who are really expert in their  fields  and it seeks very much to have  perspectives that are not  all on the left side of the political  spectrum but include a number of  republicans  one of whom is tracy meehan and has  written an excellent essay on water  management  basically making the argument we can't  afford to have such a thing as  waste water we need to be able to  recapture clean and reuse  all of our water supplies absolutely  correct  this is true in general even more true  in a world where climate change is going  to stress  uh traditional water flows uh  traditional  rainfall patterns and of course we're  seeing even in connecticut this year  the emergence of a serious drought so a  great essay on  water as a scarce resource needing to  have a price on it to incentivize  care and conservation in its management  and to encourage  investments in the sort of  infrastructure that allows us to be much  more  tight in our water systems not lose  water  now it also is the case that we  recognize  that some of the problems of the past  are a function of people not  thinking about uh ecological linkages  and ah schmitz has a great essay  on the need to understand ecosystems in  a more holistic way  understand the connections across  different aspects of the plant and  animal world  and i think this provides a very  important science foundation  for this uh 21st century approach to  environmental protection i'm imagining  and just adjacent to that argument that  ah schmitz presents  uh is another essay from uh authors that  uh  have their homes in the school of the  environment paul anastas and julie  zimmerman  who make the case for a systems  perspective  in environmental law and policy an  integrated approach  not a siloed approach arguing that the  20th century was often about  managing air and water and waste and  land in  separate legal structures and separate  incentive frameworks  and it hasn't worked out that well we've  misunderstood  the connections and in some cases just  move pollution from one form to another  most famously if you put a scrubber on a  smoke stack and it pulls the suit out of  the stack  now you have a solid waste and you put  it in the land now you have a land waste  and then when rain water comes through  it it takes it into a stream and now  you've turned it into a water  problem so that's not a very good  approach and that's the kind of thing  that  paula nastus and julie zimmerman are  calling out with their essay aimed at  systems thinking systems design and  really a much more holistic approach to  how we produce things  what kinds of products we produce and  our framework of of law and policy  in response similarly jane lubchenko  who's written a beautiful essay on  oceans  tells us that we need to get beyond our  past framework that the ocean was too  big and it wouldn't ever be polluted by  us could we couldn't affect it because  it was so vast  to the more recent view that the ocean  is doomed that it's full of plastic and  hopeless  to her argument that the ocean is  central  fundamental to life on earth and