e story  is to say governments will never do  enough  we need to make sure that there is  private capital being deployed  to be part of our land conservation  effort and he talks about  land trusts and other ways of bringing  private funds  into our land conservation agenda  and really effectively a new model for  how we're going to do  the scale of habitat protection required  for  a thriving human population as well as  plant and animal  life another of the  essays that i think is really  interesting or several of the essays  relate to the need for new approaches to  communications  uh tony lacerowicz in particular from  our yale  uh community climate change  communications project  lays out a very interesting set of views  about  how americans see the problem of climate  change what motivates them to take  action  even what words resonate with people and  i think  understanding how to communicate science  to the policy arena is again a terrible  breakdown of the past and something we  need to get much better about  going forward into the future and that  brings me to one  final essay that i just want to mention  and uh  in a book with 40 essays that i'm the  editor of  i'm not allowed to have a favorite just  as no parent can have a favorite child  but i do want to say the essay by thomas  easley has resonated in a very big and  broad way  his piece is called hip-hop  sustainability  and it reflects his reality of being a  dean in the school of the environment  but also as a hip-hop artist and the  case he makes  is for bringing the discussion around  sustainability  and including climate change in  particular the communities that have  been left out of the conversation  in his case uh the black community the  inner city community  the world that he talks to in his hip  hop music and his argument is that if  you can speak to people in their  own terms in their own way of  approaching a problem  they're much more likely to engage and  he is of course  correct so h