ey're causing  and again a more integrated world where  we're thinking in a  unified way an ecological way about  problems  recognizing connections and trade-offs  and trying to be more coherent in how we  manage that  and uh as i said it's a world that needs  more innovation  uh to get to a much cleaner future we  can't rely on the tools of the past the  frameworks of the past  uh the incentives the past we need to  move forward  and i do think that the core of the  argument  should be this new value foundational  value  that takes the right of breathers ever  more seriously  so i don't think it's the case that  society doesn't care who has  the underlying environmental rights i  think we need to lodge with all of us  the right to a clean environment uh to  make environmental justice  a more central part of our policy  framework and  with that i would argue we have a new  principle that commits to an  end of externalities every polluter  either stops their harm causing  or pays fully so that's a lot to lay on  the table  i invite now questions and comments i'm  often asked whether i see  uh a promising path whether i'm an  optimist  or whether i think all the the the  shower of bad news  uh makes me a pessimist and my answer is  i am somewhat pessimistic in the short  term but in the long term i think  there's lots of opportunities and as  this book demonstrates  lots of paths forward so with that let  me stop and invite your questions your  comments your pushback  and let's have a dialogue  thank you so much dan for this wonderful  talk  and please feel free to start uh  typing up um your questions in the chat  uh tai has kindly offered to moderate  and help us handle the questions  and if i may use my provocative as the  organizer to ask the first question  um so dan i i see that you are  optimistic at the policy level  in terms of the interventions that can  be done  and that will be very very effective i  was just wondering  whether you are optimistic in terms 