t a  stop sign saying don't do that  don't pollute can't go there and uh that  sort of red light strategy did change  behavior in some regards  but it was not very good at getting  business  to become solutions providers so we  don't have  a transformed energy foundation for our  society compared to what we thought we  might have  in the way of transformation over the  past 50 years  so i want to argue that we should have  just as we have at any intersection  not just red lights that tell people  when to stop but green lights that tell  people when to go  we need the same framework of green  lights as a complement to red lights  in environmental regulation and what i  mean really is that we need incentives  we need to  get people who have the capacity to  solve problems  to step up to those issues to become  solutions providers and i think  my argument is that we need to really  structure incentives  to flow society's resources towards  those solutions providers  and frankly to steer money away from  companies that are a cause of problems  and are not moving us  towards a sustainable future so here's  the  the issues that i see us potentially uh  being able to step  up to um first i think there is a value  in making people pay for the harm they  cause  so the biggest green light is to have  those who are doing things in a clean  way  not polluting not pay and that's a  signal  of strong value in the business world  so again my number one green light is  price  signals and that would tell us if you're  doing things the right way you don't pay  if you're a polluter you're going to pay  for every increment of harm  you're putting out into society and this  is best done if we switch from  the 20th century approach to  environmental protection  it was largely what we in the business  call command  and control regulation uh largely around  government mandates that you can't  exceed a certain level of pollution  but everyone was allowed to pollute up  to that level for fre