ng the behavior of the  polluter  but also inspiring others to help  polluters  stop polluting so it's really about not  just incentivizing more  care with the production activities of  society but also to spur  innovation and investment in  energy efficiency in uh clean energy  technology  in renewable power generation uh so  again if that is what can be done  without paying a price there will be a  relative price  signal to steer people away from fossil  fuel burning  towards these new cleaner more renewable  technologies  another source of green lights can be  information  so another part of the 21st century  sustainability strategy that i want to  sketch out  for you is how we use data  and information and the capacity to get  our hands on that information  manage it and make it actionable with  information technology  in ways that were unimaginable a couple  of decades ago  and in our better planet book are  several essays  that talk about how to do this in one  way or another one of the areas that  i've been working on  through the yale initiative on  sustainable finance which i co-direct  with  my school of management colleague todd  court is  centered on better metrics  of corporate sustainability performance  so asking companies to give us more data  on their environmental social and  governance  performance in the marketplace and that  is talked about in the investment world  by its acronym esg reporting  and right now some companies report but  the data is not very good  a lot of it's backward-looking not  forward-looking a lot of it's narrowly  focused on risk and not always focused  on who's a solutions provider therefore  upside opportunities as well as kind of  the downside burdens a company is  causing  a lot of it is very focused on  reputation rather than  operational success so there's a lot of  problems with an existing framework so  todd and i have been arguing and his  essay in the better planet book makes  clear  that we would benefit as a soc