s  that i very much like  uh the one by carter roberts who's the  head of the world wildlife fund  and he has written about kind of the  need for a new business  business ethos and that actually is  capturing a spirit of the moment  suddenly there's a lot of talk that the  old business  mission of thinking that the goal is  always to maximize profits  or to deliver what the famous university  of chicago economist once called  shareholder primacy is giving way  to a new business spirit that focuses on  stakeholder responsibility  and that means that a business is not  just delivering goods  uh and profits for its shareholders its  owners  but has some responsibility to its  employees to the communities it operates  in to its customers to its suppliers  and i think uh that argument very much  is what carter roberts  is uh saying in his essay in this better  planet book  i believe that there is a societal value  change  underway that is basically saying to the  business community  you need to be more broad-minded it  can't simply be  all about profits and certainly not  about profits  where you are privatizing gains and  socializing putting on all of us the  costs of things like your pollution  so better information makes that  possible we've got a new capacity with  data  and information technology to track  who's putting those emissions out  and again in my reimagined world making  those people pay for the harms they're  causing  now it is also the case that with better  data  and tracking we can manage a whole set  of other  sustainability or environmental problems  better and another of the essays that in  the better planet book i find really  inspiring in its uh  nod to where the future might go is the  one by my environment school colleague  marion certo  on waste management and marian some of  you may know has been in that arena  for something like 30 years uh and has  really been  on the front lines of thinking about how  we do recapture and recycling of  materials  and how we try to minimize waste uh and  that's a  booming issue of societal concern uh  obviously around things like plastic but  even more broadly  and so marion lays out with one of her  uh students  a beautiful argument for a price-based  strategy of waste management where you  make people pay for what they throw away  but allow them to recycle for free and  uh  she's got a a well-structured essay  on how it is we might reimagine waste  management to really ensure that we get  to a more  closed-loop use of materials where that  which can be taken back  recaptured and reused will be and where  we're minimizing that which goes out  uh forever as waste likewise there's a  great essay in the book from tracy  meehan  uh and by the way one of the notable  things about this book is  these are not essays from  environmentalists they're from  environmental analysts  so people who are really expert in their  fields  and it seeks very much to have  perspectives that are not  all on the left side of the political  spectrum but include a number of  republicans  one of whom is tracy meehan and has  written an excellent essay on water  management  basically making the argument we can't  afford to have such a thing as  waste water we need to be able to  recapture clean and reuse  all of our water supplies absolutely  correct  this is true in general even more true  in a world where climate change is going  to stress  uh traditional water flows uh  traditional  rainfall patterns and of course we're  seeing even in connecticut this year  the emergence of a serious drought so a  great essay on  water as a scarce resource needing to  have a price on it to incentivize  care and conservation in its management  and to encourage  investments in the sort of  infrastructure that allows us to be much  more  tight in our water systems not lose  water  now it also is the case that we  recognize  that some of the problems of the past  are a function of people not  thinking about uh eco