e story  is to say governments will never do  enough  we need to make sure that there is  private capital being deployed  to be part of our land conservation  effort and he talks about  land trusts and other ways of bringing  private funds  into our land conservation agenda  and really effectively a new model for  how we're going to do  the scale of habitat protection required  for  a thriving human population as well as  plant and animal  life another of the  essays that i think is really  interesting or several of the essays  relate to the need for new approaches to  communications  uh tony lacerowicz in particular from  our yale  uh community climate change  communications project  lays out a very interesting set of views  about  how americans see the problem of climate  change what motivates them to take  action  even what words resonate with people and  i think  understanding how to communicate science  to the policy arena is again a terrible  breakdown of the past and something we  need to get much better about  going forward into the future and that  brings me to one  final essay that i just want to mention  and uh  in a book with 40 essays that i'm the  editor of  i'm not allowed to have a favorite just  as no parent can have a favorite child  but i do want to say the essay by thomas  easley has resonated in a very big and  broad way  his piece is called hip-hop  sustainability  and it reflects his reality of being a  dean in the school of the environment  but also as a hip-hop artist and the  case he makes  is for bringing the discussion around  sustainability  and including climate change in  particular the communities that have  been left out of the conversation  in his case uh the black community the  inner city community  the world that he talks to in his hip  hop music and his argument is that if  you can speak to people in their  own terms in their own way of  approaching a problem  they're much more likely to engage and  he is of course  correct so his ability to broaden the  discussion bring people into  a political dialogue that they didn't  know they had an important reason to be  part of  is i think quite stunning so let me  close just by saying  uh a few more visions of the 21st  century strategy  for a sustainable future that the better  planet book  lays out uh we had a very top-down  approach to policy in the 20th century  we need to have some top-down uh  priorities and targets set  but with bottom-up implementation  recognizing not all the work will be  done by the federal government  some will be done at the state level  some at the local level and oh by the  way some will be done at the global  level  over it all we do have the opportunity  to build on information technologies  that weren't available  in the past century we do have enormous  potential  to bring data and data tracking  technologies to bear  to give us new tools and new  possibilities for how to limit harm  particularly the idea of tracing and  tracking  emissions and putting a price on those  and frankly i would argue that the  environmental arena is  curiously unaffected up till now  by the entire revolution of information  technology  no other arena of society that i'm aware  of  has done so little to bring information  technology to bear  you know no baseball team today picks  players the way they might have  uh 30 years ago when a scout would be  chewing tobacco and saying well that guy  has good stuff  and this guy doesn't no they're data  driven they now measure  how hard people hit how fast they run  how quickly they jump  um so it's a data driven world and that  same spirit  just hasn't been deeply embedded into  the environmental world  yet but there is a potential to do it  now  as i said we're going to need to move  away from government mandates as the  core of our regulatory framework  the government pricing of harms letting  people be flexible in what they choose  to do about that  but pay fully for every bit of harm  they're causing  and again a more integrated world where  we're thinking in a  unified way an ecological way about  problems  recognizing connections and trade-offs  and trying to be more coherent in how we  manage that  and uh as i said it's a world that needs  more innovation  uh to get to a much cleaner future we  can't rely on the tools of the past the  frameworks of the past  uh the incentives the past we need to  move forward  and i do think that the core of the  argument  should be this new value foundational  value  that takes the right of breathers ever  more seriously  so i don't think it's the case that  society doesn't care who has  the underlying environmental rights i  think we need to lodge with all of us  the right to a clean environment uh to  make environmental justice  a more central part of our policy  framework and  with that i would argue we have a new  principle that commits to an  end of externalities every polluter  either stops their harm causing  or pays fully so that's a lot to lay on  the table  i invite now questions and comments i'm  often asked whether i see  uh a promising path whether i'm an  optimist  or whether i think all the the the  shower of bad news  uh makes me a pessimist and my answer is  i am somewhat pessimistic in the short  term but in the long term i think  there's lots of opportunities and as  this book demonstrates  lots of paths forward so with that let  me stop and invite your questions your  comments your pushback  and let's have a dialogue  thank you so much dan for this wonderful  talk  and please feel free to start uh  typing up um your questions in the chat  uh tai has kindly offered to moderate  and help us handle the questions  and if i may use my provocative as the  organizer to ask the first question  um so dan i i see that you are  optimistic at the policy level  in terms of the interventions that can  be done  and that will be very very effective i  was just wondering  whether you are optimistic in terms of  the  at the individual level because you know  we are seeing a rampant  uh denialism of science of climate  change  so how do we change people's minds  about being more open and sort of  accepting of the facts that are  unrolling in front of our eyes  so yeah and the connected question is do  you see  you know young activists like greta  tinberg  actually playing an important role going  forward  um how do you see the sort of the  activism  side of that movement as well  thank you um so priya you've raised one  of the most  depressing uh and could be  pessimism-inducing  aspects of our current moment which is  how many people seem uninterested  in science in data in rational analysis  um in having policy grounded in  facts uh and empirical analysis so it  really  uh it could be a source of enormous  distress  and i am actually distressed by that i  think however that the lesson  of our covid19 moment may well be that  if you don't pay attention to all of  that  you suffer and it's not that somebody  else suffers you will suffer your family  will suffer  um and there are kind of straightforward  ways to put your risk into a much lower  category by wearing a mask and  social distancing um and the people who  are doing that  are having a better outcome than people  or not and of course there's bad luck  people that work behaving properly that  still get the disease and  risky behavior folks who don't but over  the broad  scope of society i think it's going to  appear very clear  that the people who are following  science following data  are going to come out better but i am  stunned  at our current political moment where so  many people still  even if they have faced personal  challenges don't seem to  act in their public life in their  political life  with regard to that very straightforward  logic  so i think it is uh worrisome  i do think that we have activists who  are starting to sharpen our thinking  um and greta gets great credit for  having raised the profile of climate  change  uh her school strikes i think have  motivated lots of young people to think  this can't be left to the folks who are  of my generation your generation  to solve the problem we haven't yet  gotten there and  the the young folks i think are willing  to be more creative to try  new things um but i i do think and i  i don't know whether this is put on  greta specifically but i do think  there is also um some challenges for the  young  and i see this um you know in uh in many  contexts  but there are trade-offs one of the  reasons we still have a pandemic debate  on  is because it is a terrible thing that  people have lost their jobs  so there is an economic counterpoint  to the pandemic that is real and people  have suffered  really serious life losses because of  lost jobs lost economic  security so i think the fact is that  those who care about the environment  have to acknowledge  that most people also care about their  families economic security  uh the ability to have a job so how do  you balance  jobs in the environment uh has got to be  taken more seriously  and in that regard you know i pray i'm  going to end with your question  because i think it's the right one i i  think the answer is data and science and  good analysis that's how  how you do balancing that's how you make  trade-offs um  and i don't see any other path forward  but to dig deeper  into the science and have it emerge even  more fully and forcefully  as the foundation for our decision  making  thank you very much i think for mrs  frankie  well thank you uh to follow up with  thomas  easley's thoughts incidentally  his last name and mine are the same my  maiden name was  easily so um  has any work been done to  help with education as far as  primary and secondary schools are  concerned i  i know it is an area where our hope may  be  if they are educated along the lines  that you're proposing  so i would tell you that um almost  everyone who's thought  long and hard about what it's going to  take to get to a sustainable future  sees education and frankly in  generational  shifts in underlying understanding and  values  as foundational and i think this is true  in some arenas that we know already and  can see the evidence  you know the the world of recycling  didn't exist when you and i were young  um we had to be retrained and we're  still not always probably as good as the  younger folks i have to admit to  occasionally throwing  a a beer can in the trash and not in the  recycling bin  now my children just think that's absurd  they they would never imagine  throwing away a good aluminum can that's  ready for uh  repurposing as a new aluminum can so  that's an element of  retraining and of education where the  young have got it  clear in their minds they don't even  have to think about it where some of us  are still slow on the draw so i do think  better environmental education being  beginning at the earliest grades  deepening the understanding of science  is the foundation  is going to be quite fundamental to  success on all of this  dan a question from the chat uh we have  a couple of piled up  uh someone says thanks for raising the  issue of incorporating externalities in  our economic analyses  how do you suggest that might occur  so i think there are several ways to do  this again i'm a big believer in  bringing a much richer base of data and  analytics  to environmental policy so i would like  to have people report  more fully uh on their environmental  impacts i think we should require  companies  to provide metrics that tell us how much  in the way of greenhouse gases they're  emitting what kind of harm they're  putting out their effluent pipe into the  the rivers and streams nearby what  they're putting up a smokestack beyond  greenhouse gases  that uh might be of societal concern and  uh  with that data one can begin to put a  number on it that's why i'm saying we  need to  recast our epa away from the engineers  of the past  and toward epidemiologists ecologists  risk analysts  and economists who will do that work  of putting a number on the externalities  and then sending out the bills  making people pay for the harm they're  causing and by the way some people think  that sounds  quite radical it's actually not uh  this has been the core of environmental  efforts over  400 years which basically said going  back to some of the earliest uh  nuisance cases uh in british law  400 years ago on which american law  builds the the most famous case is uh  called  aldrid and in that case a pig farmer was  told you can't have your smelly pigs  polluting the neighborhood stinking up  the neighborhood either get rid of your  pigs stop the harm  or you're gonna have to pay your  neighbors for the harm you're causing so  this idea  of ending externalities making people  pay for their harm  is 400 years old in our anglo-american  legal tradition  many companies are producing esg goals  spontaneously  from within the organization but u.s  fossil fuel companies are singularly  refractory to such endeavors  how can they be brought around so i  actually am um not very optimistic  about voluntary environmental social  governance reporting  i think there's a problem never mind in  the fossil fuel industry but broadly  that companies tend to report uh on  their own terms and without regard to  the need for comparability  across the data one company to the next  so i'm in favor  of uh having our securities and exchange  commission  uh establish a core set of  sustainability metrics again in the  environment social and governance  categories that would be required  along alongside of financial data as  part of quarterly and annually corporate  reports  as providing the foundational  information that the stock market needs  so that people can understand  uh who's in a good position who's not um  and frankly it should gauge  those who are providing solutions to the  market so that we can say that's a  company that may be a a winner over time  as well as those who are causing harms  or holding assets that are um  that are a problem uh holding fossil  fuel assets for example  so i do think a a framework  a government structure of um  sustainability metrics is probably the  way to go  and that would of course apply first and  foremost to the fossil fuel companies  that would then have to tell us uh what  their risks are  uh and whether they're counting on  getting at all these fossil fuels they  hold  for their future success thank you  another  question there are some consequences to  removing fossil fuels  the lwcf just got full funding in the  great american outdoors act  but it is funded by royalties from oil  and gas exploration in the north sea  won't they have less money to pay their  royalties going forward  um yes and in fact that might be an  argument in  uh some might push back on my entire pay  for your externalities uh  line of reasoning because the goal over  time is actually not to have people pay  it's to have people stop polluting um  and likewise  uh to the extent that royalties from  fossil fuel exploitation  uh are what fund that um land and water  account uh we probably over time have to  plan for a day when those numbers begin  to dwindle  because the amount of fossil fuels being  extracted is going dramatically down  okay um please could you explain more  about the metrics that companies need to  have to achieve  sdg for example b lab provides some  tools  but maybe there are more that can be  built in the near future  so this raises another very good point  and let me just back up in case not  everyone's familiar with the sdg  reference but in addition in 2015 to  having the paris climate  change agreement the u.n put forward  something called the sustainable  development goals  17 different goals that represent really  not just  challenges of environment but many other  issues including ending poverty ending  hunger  moving towards clean energy providing  better public health  all of which are underpinned by metrics  so there's 169  targets that have been set that these  goals are meant to drive countries  towards addressing  over the 15 years to 2030. so i do think  there's a lot of work being done on  measuring sustainable development goals  and a good number of companies i would  say many companies that are serious  about their sustainability strategies  are trying to translate some of those  goals  into their own corporate reporting  framework so they're putting together  uh a corporate report on some of the  goals that they think are most relevant  to their own business their own industry  and putting that information out to the  world uh it may well be that  as part of the the government mandated  esg disclosure esg reporting that i  would like to see  that a a core set of those sustainable  development goals would be among the  things required  okay thank you um  comments here the call for bottom up or  top down  is still too anthropocentric this is a  recipe for collapse one of our  participants  suggests we need to transcend this  vocabulary and these metaphors  uh he followed up a bit later with this  more data and  greater amounts of science are not  enough we need to change the fundamental  metaphors our culture  is working with and uh he mentions  the fatal consequences of a misplaced  metaphor in the agricultural industry  so um first of all i do think how you  frame things matters  and again several of the essays in the  better planet book make that point  jade levchenko's uh essay on the oceans  is all about a new narrative for the  oceans  um tony elizarowitz's work on climate  change is all about  how you frame the issue in a manner that  engages more people and brings them into  the conversation  uh gets them to take the issue more  seriously um i think the chain  the challenge that the questioner is  presenting is that  it's very very difficult to uh deliver  such a radical change of perspectives  that people are going beyond their own  human interest to really uh focus as  much as maybe we should  would like them to on uh biodiversity on  the  on the world of nature as an equally  important priority  and you know my own experience in the  policy arena is that it's in fact a  failing cause to try to hope that  everyone's going to be high-minded  and take nature as seriously as human  life so instead i believe  that the best hope is to frame the  agenda around how nature supports people  and that we need to protect biodiversity  and uh support the ocean  uh because in part at least it is  essential for our own human success  thank you please could you elaborate  more about environmental justice  especially because nowadays there is an  increased role of climate litigation  in advancing and undermining climate  change action  so i do think uh at the core of my uh  vision for a 21st century  approach to environmental protection is  this idea  of not permitting any externalities that  is to say at least no pollution for  free uh there may be some unavoidable  level of uh  of harm that's going to be emitted but  it should all be fully paid for  at uh at rates that will provide ongoing  deterrence and encourage people to  continue to  move away from harm causing and i do  think more broadly  that uh what is now often talked about  is climate justice beyond environmental  justice  is suggesting that uh it's unfair to a  number of communities to have uh  the risk of climate change rising and i  like to say that climate change is  particularly challenging  because the the vectors of  responsibility are even more complicated  than with most issues  which is to say greenhouse gas emissions  blanket the earth  so there is a geographic spread of  enormous scale  no one country can solve the problem  alone even if the united states uh  entirely counterfactually were to stop  all emissions tomorrow  it would only take 15 percent of the  emissions at the global scale  out of the system we'd still have 85  remaining so it can only be addressed in  a collective  global approach and furthermore from a  point of view of climate justice  there's not only a spatial spread that's  enormous but  across time a temporal spread and  a lot of the harm that's being done is  to none not anyone who's alive today but  our children our grandchildren and their  children and grandchildren  so there is a profound intergenerational  equity dimension here as well  so i do think environmental justice is a  big topic  along several different lines including  a more immediate harm spilled from  factories onto adjacent communities  often communities that are disadvantaged  communities of color  and then these various vectors of  justice  related to the climate change problem so  lots to work through  and much of it's been under attended to  uh in the past  kind of uh environmental discussions and  empire and environmental policy  frameworks that have been in place  okay i hope i'm not missing the  beginning part of this question  uh wouldn't this additional layer of  sustainability metrics result in another  level of corporate government  governance and middle management  no i think um the requirement that  companies report on the environment is  something that most  companies of any scale are doing already  they're just not doing it in a coherent  cross-company cross-industry comparable  way  so i think it could in fact be  simplifying  for companies to have a single reporting  framework  defined by the government right now  there's a dozen different private sector  uh groups out there in the data  analytics world trying to collect this  data  sending out surveys uh pushing companies  to respond to their  uh survey and and as a result there's a  a scramble within companies to keep up  with all these requests  so a single unified government structure  would i think actually  cut the amount of time and effort  required and improve the quality and  comparability of the data  in a way that would benefit maybe not  every company but certainly all the  investors  who want that framework of comparable  high quality high integrity data  that they can actually trust and would  view as a foundation  for making investment decisions  okay uh continued comment from other one  of the participants  we now need to move beyond the major  universities and change public discourse  in  culture other rhodes scholars have been  working on this for a while  and he suggests uh i guess you're  familiar with them  so what is the question there uh i guess  just the comment about moving beyond the  major universities and changing public  discourse  maybe more just of a common um you know  i think there is um  uh obviously a case to have uh the the  conversation carried beyond universities  to be taken into the public domain into  the political arena  i think again as i mentioned earlier  what's notable about this year's  presidential election  is the degree to which environment  broadly but climate change in particular  has been part of the debate much more so  in the past  and i do think again we can credit some  of the younger  activists for having driven the agenda  uh and for having ensured that the  candidates really in both parties  um are having to step up and answer  questions  and i think that's given the public as  clear a  signal and has sharply defined a choice  on that set of issues as we have seen in  in any recent election  thank you i think we've covered the  questions that have been submitted thus  far  i'm just checking for any new ones but i  think  that's pretty much it good well it's  been a pleasure to be part of this  conversation um  i thank priya and the entire team at the  frankie center for uh  the opportunity to be uh with you today  and to have this discussion  and uh hope some of you will carry it  forward in your own way and in your own  uh  settings uh in the weeks and months  ahead  thank you so much dan for 